First, we convert the total concert time into minutes to work with consistent units. A 2-hour concert is:
$$
2 \text{ hours} \times 60 \text{ minutes/hour} = 120 \text{ minutes}
$$

Next, we account for the 10-minute intermission. Subtracting the intermission time from the total concert time gives the time available for performances:
$$
120 \text{ minutes} - 10 \text{ minutes} = 110 \text{ minutes}
$$

Each group requires the following time:
- 2 minutes to get on stage
- 6 minutes to perform
- 2 minutes to exit the stage

Adding these up gives the total time per group:
$$
2 \text{ minutes} + 6 \text{ minutes} + 2 \text{ minutes} = 10 \text{ minutes}
$$

Now, we divide the remaining time (110 minutes) by the time per group (10 minutes to calculate how many groups can perform):
$$
110 \text{ minutes} \div 10 \text{ minutes/group} = 11 \text{ groups}
$$

Therefore, the total number of groups that can perform is:
$$
\boxed{11}
$$
